# Line endings, pinned.
#
# The booth laptop is Windows, where Git's default (core.autocrlf=true) checks
# files out with CRLF endings. `npm run build` — which "Start Booth.cmd" runs on
# every launch — writes public/*.html with LF. So every single launch left those
# two files "modified" against a checkout that only differed in invisible
# characters, and the next `git pull` refused to run:
#
#     error: Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten
#            by merge: public/index.html, public/standalone.html
#
# Pinning everything to LF means the file on disk matches the file in the repo
# byte for byte, so the build no longer dirties the tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two exceptions. Windows' batch interpreter is genuinely unhappy with LF
# endings in some constructs, and binaries must never be touched at all.
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ico  binary
*.gcode text eol=lf
